his result requires specific settings for the stochasticity parameters, notably that the exploration parameter $\\eta_1$η 1​ for unobserved directions must scale with the step size (e.g., $\\eta_1 = \\delta^{-p}$η 1​=δ−p for $1/2 < p < 2/3$1/2<p<2/3).\n\nTheorem 2 generalizes this result to measurement matrices $A$A with arbitrary non-zero singular values. By utilizing the proposed correction term in the update rule, the algorithm still achieves asymptotic posterior consistency. This provides a theoretical foundation that was previously missing for most diffusion-based inverse solvers.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEfficient Bayesian Inference for Discretely Observed Continuous Time Markov Chains (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/393923770_Efficient_Bayesian_Inference_for_Discretely_Observed_Continuous_Time_Markov_Chains/fulltext/6880550b1a77b36b5b04ac51/Efficient-Bayesian-Inference-for-Discretely-Observed-Continuous-Time-Markov-Chains.pdf)\nciteturn962search15 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; By constructing a pseudo-likelihood over both the transition matrix P and its spectral generator L, we offer a principled solution to the embedding problem that preserves probabilistic structure while enabling scalable inference. ... We prove a BvM theorem for the transition matrix and establish posterior consistency for the spectral decomposition of L.Through empirical studies on synthetic and diffusion-based systems, we demonstrate the method’s capacity to recover and quantify uncertainty in the dynamics.Our approach relies on two main assumptions: regular observation times and a biorthogonal generator L.\nPosterior standard deviations (bottom row of Figure 3) are largest in the transition regions at basin boundaries, where transitions are rare but not negligible. Notably, uncertainty in P̃ is smaller overall (note the different legend scale), again reflecting regularization through the spectral approximation. The final column in Figure 3 shows the posterior summaries for the generator matrix L. The posterior mean exhibits dominant diagonal structure and sparse off-diagonal mass. Posterior uncertainty is concentrated along the diagonal, while off-diagonal transition regions, where rates are near zero, exhibit greater certainty.\n\n6 Conclusions\n\nWe present a pseudo-Bayesian framework for inference on CTMCs observed at discrete time points, addressing the challenge posed by the intractability of the true likelihood. By constructing a pseudo-likelihood over both the transition matrix P and its spectral generator L, we offer a principled solution to the embedding problem that preserves probabilistic structure while enabling scalable inference. The resulting Gibbs sampler respects the constraints required for embeddability and yields coherent posterior uncertainty. Our method is straightforward to implement, computationally efficient, and theoretically grounded. We prove a BvM theorem for the transition matrix and establish posterior consistency for the spectral decomposition of L. Through empirical studies on synthetic and diffusion-based systems, we demonstrate the method’s capacity to recover and quantify uncertainty in the dynamics. Our approach relies on two main assumptions: regular observation times and a biorthogonal generator L. Future work could explore relaxing one or both of these\n\n18--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGEPC: Group-Equivariant Posterior Consistency (https://payshopp.net/?_=%2Fpdf%2F2602.00191%23oiIsiUa7YGXxkxUiKzZPY60%3D)\nciteturn962search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; GEPC: Group-Equivariant Posterior Consistency ... 2025) are complementary to GEPC: they refine matrix- ... GEPC multi-modal diffusion models.\nNLL\n0.091\n0.574\n0.521\n0.990\n0.999\n0.992\n0.814\n0.105\n0.786\n0.652\n1000F + 0J\nIC (diffusion)\n0.921\n0.516\n0.519\n0.080\n0.028\n0.100\n0.485\n0.972\n0.510\n0.459\n